WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.333
A network API plays the role of an interface
between programs that do not share a process.

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:10.666
The caller needs to know what operation is
available, how to supply its inputs, and how to

00:00:10.666 --> 00:00:12.385
interpret success or failure.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.684
It should not need to know the implementation
language or internal database schema. Consider

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:22.474
a weather service: a client requests a forecast
for a named location, and the service

00:00:22.474 --> 00:00:25.291
returns structured data with a documented
meaning.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.717
The diagram distinguishes the resource
identifier, request parameters, and response

00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:36.123
representation. These pieces form a contract
that lets a browser application, a mobile

00:00:36.123 --> 00:00:41.718
application, or another backend use the same
service independently of its implementation.

00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:47.244
HTTP provides an existing envelope for that
contract. A request selects a target and method,

00:00:47.244 --> 00:00:52.531
supplies fields such as authorization or
accepted media types, and may carry a body. The

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:57.877
service responds with an appropriate status and
a representation whose content type tells

00:00:57.877 --> 00:00:59.559
the client how to decode it.

00:00:59.718 --> 00:01:04.628
For a forecast endpoint, a successful JSON
response and an unknown-location error are

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:09.653
different documented outcomes. The diagram
branches on the service's result rather than

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:14.678
assuming every response contains usable forecast
data. A client that ignores status and

00:01:14.678 --> 00:01:19.877
attempts to parse every body as a forecast can
turn a clear service error into a confusing

00:01:19.877 --> 00:01:21.032
application failure.

00:01:21.218 --> 00:01:26.208
A client sends a write and waits, but the
connection fails before it receives a response.

00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:30.919
The server may have completed the write; the
client simply cannot tell. Retrying can

00:01:30.919 --> 00:01:33.835
therefore repeat an operation that already
happened.

00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:39.116
Compare two ways to create a document. A PUT to
a client-chosen identifier replaces the

00:01:39.116 --> 00:01:44.458
representation at that identifier, so repeating
the same intended operation has the same

00:01:44.458 --> 00:01:49.557
effect on that resource. A POST to a collection
may ask the server to allocate a new

00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:53.138
identifier each time, creating two documents
after a retry.

00:01:53.335 --> 00:01:58.663
The diagram follows the lost response, then
contrasts the stable target with two newly

00:01:58.663 --> 00:01:59.779
allocated targets.

00:01:59.835 --> 00:02:04.543
The important distinction is the intended
effect, not whether repeated responses have

00:02:04.543 --> 00:02:09.418
identical bytes or status codes. Deleting an
existing resource and deleting it again can

00:02:09.418 --> 00:02:14.293
leave the same final absence even if the second
response reports that nothing was found.

00:02:14.335 --> 00:02:19.767
A safe read does not request a state change; an
idempotent write can request a change whose

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:25.140
repetition has the same intended effect. For an
operation such as charging an account, the

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.215
API can define an idempotency key and atomically
associate that key with one accepted

00:02:30.215 --> 00:02:30.633
result.

00:02:30.835 --> 00:02:36.028
The illustration shows a repeated key returning
the recorded result rather than initiating a

00:02:36.028 --> 00:02:40.713
second charge, provided the documented retention
and parameter rules are satisfied.
